# Crontab for the trading agent. Install with `deploy/install.sh`, or paste the
# line below into `crontab -e` with PROJECT_DIR replaced by the real path.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE WINDOW IS DELIBERATELY TOO WIDE
#
# The US market trades 09:30-16:00 Eastern, which is 13:30-20:00 UTC in summer
# and 14:30-21:00 UTC in winter. Encoding that precisely means the schedule is
# wrong twice a year, and wrong in a way nothing reports -- the agent simply
# stops trading for the first hour of every day and no error is raised.
#
# So cron fires generously, 13:00-20:59 UTC, and the market-open gate decides
# whether there is anything to do. A pass outside market hours reads the clock,
# finds it closed, and exits in about a second having spent nothing: no model
# call, no order, no tokens. The authority on whether the market is open is the
# broker, which is the only thing that actually knows about half-days and
# holidays.
#
# Deliberately trading correctness for a handful of no-op runs a day.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHICH MODE
#
# The line below runs in DRY RUN: every decision is made, journalled and
# announced, and Alpaca validates each order without placing it. Add --live to
# the end to place orders for real. That word is the entire difference between
# a rehearsal and a competition entry, which is why it lives here in plain
# sight rather than in a config file.

# Pins the hours below to UTC no matter what the server's clock is set to.
# Without this they mean local time, and a schedule that runs at the wrong hour
# looks exactly like a schedule that runs.
CRON_TZ=UTC
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
SHELL=/bin/bash

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO SCHEDULES, AND WHY THE MINUTES MUST NOT OVERLAP
#
# The full pass costs a model call per candidate. The exits-only pass costs a
# few broker reads and no tokens at all, so it runs between the full ones. The
# gap between passes is how far a position can travel unwatched: measured over
# 81 round trips, losses that overshot the stop while the market was open cost
# $3,847 beyond where the rule said to exit.
#
# Both lines take the same per-profile lock, which is what keeps them from
# colliding. It is also why the second line lists minutes explicitly instead of
# */5 -- if both fired on the same minute, the exits pass could take the lock
# first and the full pass would skip, so entries would quietly stop happening.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# min                      hour   dom mon dow   command
*/15                       13-20  *   *   1-5   PROJECT_DIR/deploy/run_pass.sh
5,10,20,25,35,40,50,55     13-20  *   *   1-5   PROJECT_DIR/deploy/run_pass.sh --exits-only

# For live trading, add --live to the end of both lines.
